WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.730 --> 00:00:03.389
You know, of all the established directors working today

00:00:03.389 --> 00:00:07.020
I think the one that would feel 
most at home in my world,

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:10.160
the world of the video essay, is Edgar Wright.

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:14.960
See, basically what I do here every week, like any essayist, is make an argument.

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:19.260
The difference between me and say,
 New York Times Columnist Paul Krugman

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:23.200
besides a PhD in Economics and like, accolades

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:26.920
is that I use video to make my arguments, 
where he has only language

00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:32.000
But even so, our goals are the same: to make a persuasive and engaging argument.

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:35.500
And just like writers pay close attention 
to the flow of their words,

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:39.140
carefully crafting the transitions 
from one idea to the next

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:43.960
I see the rhythm and momentum of my videos as crucial to their effectiveness

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:47.580
And here's where I can learn a lot from Edgar Wright.

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:48.980
[Scott]: Can I get back to you on that?

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:50.480
(bell ringing)

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:53.320
Edgar wright is a master of rhythm.

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:58.580
What you just saw is one of many extraordinary scene transitions from Scott Pilgrim vs. The World

00:00:58.580 --> 00:01:00.800
It lasts about four and a half seconds,

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:03.920
But it's worth taking a second, slower look

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:07.060
This transition is all about movement to the right

00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:11.939
It kicks off with Anna Kendrick looking to Camera Right, which is the direction of the movement

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:15.060
But she's also looking at her brother through the split screen

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:19.140
Her split screen is keyframed off 
to the left and so is Scott Pilgrim's

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:22.560
But Wright has cleverly positioned him left in the frame

00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:27.300
So he can bring in the unseen right-hand part of the shot to extend the dolly wipe

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:32.780
And the right of that frame is the armchair which wipes across to reveal the next outdoor setting

00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:40.980
And the onomatopoeia letters of the school bell aid this transition into a shot that continues the motion by actually dollying to the right.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:44.340
If you were to diagram out this transition, it would look a little like this

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:49.500
and it gives you a better sense of just how much care is put into a handful of frames.

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:56.200
The transitions are a site of opportunity for Edgar Wright, they're not only an opportunity for visual jokes,

00:01:56.200 --> 00:02:00.380
as my friend Tony explored briefly in another video which I'll link to right here

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:05.820
They're a chance to build important connective tissue that brings the viewer through the story.

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:11.380
In fact I think a story is a lot like an argument. It has to be persuasive to work.

00:02:11.380 --> 00:02:12.680
[Stephen]: Scott!
[Scott]: What?

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:15.540
[Stephen]: You only played one note for that entire song

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:18.280
In the beginning of this movie, Scott Pilgrim is adrift.

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:23.760
The break-up that defined his life for the past year is no longer an excuse for his sour attitude

00:02:23.769 --> 00:02:30.759
He begins a relationship with a 17-year old that's clearly not serious to him and he's kind of just floating through life

00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:35.120
It's a feeling that I'm sure a lot of people are familiar with, I know I am.

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:40.720
When everything sort of melts together and you skip around in time from place to place.

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:46.080
To communicate this feeling, Wright melts together the transitions between settings

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:50.500
He utilizes a few techniques to do this; 
on Scott's date with knives for example

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:55.800
There are a series of frame wipes that use diegetic moving objects, usually people,

00:02:55.800 --> 00:03:00.700
that are so seamless they momentarily trick the brain into not registering the transition.

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:05.260
What's more these wipes are all accompanied by sound effects which are also diegetic

00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:09.660
Whether it's a car, a skateboard, 
the rustling of clothes hangers,

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:13.720
or the beep of a sliding door 
all pulling you deeper into the world.

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:21.460
You also have shots where the reverse shot is matched in framing, but in a totally different location.

00:03:21.460 --> 00:03:25.800
I mean this is really cool because Wright is using the rules of cinema against you

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:29.919
you're not trained to think that a scene transition could come between a shot reverse shot

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:35.580
So you're already engaging with the next place before you realize that you've left the last one

00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:43.580
When you think back on this sequence It's hard to remember all the locations as distinct, which is exactly what Scott is feeling.

00:03:43.580 --> 00:03:44.920
[Stephen]: I told you like 50 times

00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:46.400
[Kim]: We're going to this party, retard

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:52.660
All throughout Scott pilgrim, Wright never misses a chance to use a creative transition, whether it's from scene to scene

00:03:52.660 --> 00:03:54.520
[Scott]: Oh, hey, it's tonight at the R-

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:57.720
Or within scenes, from shot to shot

00:03:57.720 --> 00:03:59.000
[Scott]: Wallace!

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:01.040
I think the overall effect of this is two-fold.

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:06.720
Not only do the wipes and whip pans and match cuts draw you along the story as it unfolds,

00:04:06.860 --> 00:04:10.200
But they also make you into a more observant viewer.

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:16.160
There are transitions that disappear the first time you watch, and stand out the second time or vice versa

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:20.040
I mean where exactly is the scene transition here?

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:24.060
It's happening completely off-screen, 
or rather, behind the screen.

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:29.460
My guess is either that the house set is on wheels or the bathroom itself is being spun around to face another backdrop.

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:33.160
Whichever it is, the transition is 
disorienting in the best way,

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:39.080
and because what's happening isn't possible, you're encouraged to sort of put the film together,

00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:43.040
an interactivity that's appropriate
 in a movie about gaming.

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:48.380
What's important is that the film goer is involved and engrossed at the same time.

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:57.700
This delicate balance is achieved through transitions that are often lyrical, like a kind of visual poetry in the most unlikely place.

00:04:57.700 --> 00:05:05.060
It might be worth noting too that Scott Pilgrim is a story about a transitionary period In the hero's life.

00:05:05.060 --> 00:05:07.900
A period between knowing who you were

00:05:07.900 --> 00:05:10.300
and deciding who you're going to be

00:05:10.580 --> 00:05:16.540
Life's transitions are harder to navigate 
than those in film or a video essay,

00:05:16.540 --> 00:05:27.380
but all of them have to be persuasive,
and persuasion is about more than the words or the images or the ideas you use

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:29.860
It's also about how you connect now.

